ty he says right now his concern  is for the people of tonga but that  there is an importance to understanding  what happened because it could happen  again  the tonga volcano has a major eruption  about every thousand years but when it  happens it doesn't just happen once  there's a very good chance that we will  have renewed eruptions maybe not of the  same size the same scale but  this could certainly go on for for quite  a few years  now figuring out the sequence of events  is going to be important starting with  when the volcano started popping out of  the water again in december of 2014 it  created an island between two islands  after several months it stabilized  enough that there were expeditions two  french explorers who were sailing past  the island on their worldwide voyage  became nasa's eyes and ears collecting  some of the very first images and  samples of the interior island now if  this volcano seems small that's because  only a fraction of it was visible it was  actually massive the part that could be  seen was just a small portion of the rim  around the crater it's it's your iceberg  you know the vast majority of this  volcano is underwater now last month the  volcano started puffing again letting  out pressure it was being watched this  satellite image was just prior to the  eruption and seems to show the middle  part of the island had slipped back  underwater this disappearance is very  interesting volcanologist john stix says  there are theories as to what happened  it could be that the recent puffing was  just pressure building and that led to  the eruption but the fact the island  disappeared may mean something else the  puffing could have been the magma  chamber letting out pressure until there  wasn't enough to hold up the roof it  could have collapsed leading to the  island slipping underwater and then the  eruption so i think time will tell if  this is the case or or not but i think  it's definitely at least a possibility  at this stage  understanding what happened will take  time satellites only tell a part of the  picture to see underwater and what's  left we'll take ships and sonar  equipment re-mapping the ocean floor  for now getting close enough to do that  is still too dangerous  mike armstrong global news  tributes are pouring in for a revered  zen buddhist monk who helped spread the  practice of mindfulness in the west  tiknot han died peacefully in vietnam at  the age of 95. he was a prominent  buddhist monk peace activist and leading  voice in the op in opposition to the  vietnam war he was exiled from vietnam  for 39 years over his mission to end the  conflict tiknathan lived much of his  adult life at a retreat he founded in  france but spent his final years in  vietnam at the monastery where he was  ordained today the dalai lama tweeted  the best way we can pay tribute to him  is to continue his work to promote peace  in the world  next how canadians are honoring the  remarkable careers of two women decades  after they first made history  [Music]  well she's known as one of the greatest  jazz singers ever to grace the stage in  canada and now eleanor collins is making  history once again the 102 year old  legend is being honored by canada post  with her very own stamp as katherine  ercart reports it's the latest  distinction in colin's decades-long  career  eleanor collins is a canadian legend  [Music]  have you seen the girl  known as the first lady of jazz  game now 102 years old she has received  another honor canada post has unveiled a  stamp featuring collins  i feel  wonderful  and honored  you know at 102 years old  one doesn't expect to even be remembered  born in alberta collins started  performing in the 1930s later starring  in the eleanor show making her the first  black woman to headline a tv show in  north america over the years she  performed with greats like dizzy  gillespie and oscar peterson more  recently  [Music]  she shared the stage with musician  marcus mosley she carries herself with  such dignity and with such grace and  when she stands on that stage and starts  to sing  something comes alive and you're just  you're enraptured  she's gifted she's talented and she  knows what she knows how to use that  those gifts that she has  collins also faced racism when she moved  to bc white neighbors once petitioned  against her black family living in the  same area in response collins  volunteered as a music teacher at her  children's school to help break down  barriers  to really have someone affirm  your work and life  on a postage stamp  [Music]  that is something the new stamp of  collins was illustrated by david  beliveau friends say it's about time she  received the honor and they're grateful  it has been released during her lifetime  i love her i love her forthrightness her  honesty her commitment to  believing in the positive she's a living  example of that philosophy i'm hungry  catherine cart global news  vancouver  that is global national for this  saturday night i'm sophie louie  tonight we leave you with a special step  back in time to celebrate canada's giant  leap forward in space history 30 years  ago today dr roberta bondar blasted off  becoming canada's first woman and the  world's first neurologist in space  the emotion of actually seeing it i'm a  scientist right we all learn that the  earth is the third rock from the sun but  seeing it in real time getting above and  seeing the edge of the planet and having  it rotate silently below the window  it was amazing to look off and see  starlight that doesn't twinkle and to  see an unfathomable black those are all  the things that i remember  bondar spent eight days on board nasa's  space shuttle discovery conducting a  groundbreaking study to this day her  work up there and at home has helped  cultivate curiosity and inspire  generations of canadians  thanks for watching and have a good  night  [Music]
 

